; Tutorial 1.01: Hello, World!
;
; Everything after a semicolon is a comment. The compiler skips it, so comments are where these tutorials explain
; what the code does. Read the file from top to bottom, then run it.
;
; A Blitz program runs from the first line to the last. There is no "main" function you have to write: the code
; at the top level of the file is the program.

; Print writes a line of text and moves to the next line. If no graphics mode is open yet, Blitz opens a small
; window for the text.
Print "Hello, World!"

; Write is like Print, but stays on the same line.
Write "This is "
Write "one line, "
Print "written in three parts."

; An empty Print leaves a blank line.
Print

; Numbers can be printed too. Blitz turns them into text on its own.
Print 42
Print 1 + 2 * 3

; Text and numbers are joined with +. The number is turned into text first.
Print "Two plus two is " + (2 + 2)

Print
Print "Press any key to quit."

; WaitKey stops the program until a key is pressed. Without it the window would close right away.
WaitKey

; End stops the program. The end of the file does the same, so End is only needed to stop somewhere in the middle.
End
